WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.750 align:start position:0%
 
kate green um  if i can just ask you on covid  um what do you make of what matt hancock  has just been saying  do you think do you think we're heading  towards uh ending lockdown  on june 21st and are you happy about  that  well it's a very difficult a very finely  balanced decision  for the government and it's absolutely  right that it's taken on the basis of  all the  available data and that it's  transparently taken and that it's robust  uh obviously we're all keen to see  things get as near to normal as possible  as quickly as possible but that must be  done with public health in mind  and so i would say if we have to  maintain some protective measures  beyond the 21st of june that is what the  government should do but um  i think it is really important as a  decision taken on the basis of the data  but knowing what you do now what you  know now  um would you basically go along with the  noise that's coming from government at  the moment which is  there is no reason yet to  deviate from the road map which  essentially  heads to lifting all the restrictions  on june the 21st what i'm hearing  different experts say is that it's very  finely balanced that it's not a  clear-cut decision at this stage  so i don't think i'm in a position to  say today and i don't think the  government should be in a position to  say today  what will be the answer on june the 21st  i think it's a very  finely balanced decision um it's one  that's got to be taken with the benefit  of  all the data on one that the government  has to be absolutely open and  transparent about  and accountable for are you going to  welcome  the prime minister's call to jab  the world by the end of next year  i'm afraid i didn't hear that trevor  sorry i asked you if  uh labor is going to welcome the prime  minister's call to the g7  to have the whole world jabbed uh by  the end of ninth of night of next year  2022.  yes i think that's a really good call  because we've all known that unless  everybody in the world is safe nobody is  fully safe and of course  while i really welcome the progress that  we've made in this country with rolling  out the vaccination program the nhs our  scientists have done a wonderful  job and we obviously still have and  people who need to  receive the vaccine here and it's very  important that for example in the areas  where we're seeing  a rise in the delta infection uh that we  have surge vaccination programs put in  place and effective but  i do think that it is absolutely our  obligation um  as global as members of the global  community to make sure that we  are getting vaccines to people in the  countries that desperately need them  and making sure that those people are  safe because that's the right thing to  do morally  and it's in the interests of everybody  in the world and including in this  country  on education um you've criticized the  government over its plans  for a catch-up program and so kevin  collins uh  who you've supported this week said that  the government  would need to spend something like 15  billion pounds  um would you support kevin collins's  program for an extended school day which  is what  most of the money would have been spent  on  well we've put forward our own proposals  which also include  an activities after the end of the  formal school day and breakfast clubs  before the start of the formal school  day  and i very much welcome the suggestions  that kevin collins is making many of  them  are very similar to the proposals that  we are putting forward  we know that activities you know art  sports drama and so on  um schools have wonderful facilities to  offer it's a shame for those facilities  to shut up shop at 3 30 when children  could continue to benefit from them we  know that children's emotional and  social well-being is as  important to parents and teachers right  now as as making up lost learning  so those activities will give them a  real chance to to enjoy their childhoods  to  to rebuild that social well-being but  also of course  it will benefit children and young  people's learning  and so yes um i think kevin cohen's  proposals are very good  and i do very much welcome the proposal  to use extra time at the beginning of  the end of the school day to offer  uh those additional activities your  proposals  are priced at about 14.7 billion  pounds from the document you've issued  collins says 15 billion over the next  three years  okay they overlap but um is the shadow  chancellor  rachel reeves okay with you wanting to  spend something north of  20 billion pounds of public money over  the next three years  is that labour policy so these proposals  signed off by rachel and the shadow  treasury team with the support of the  entire shadow cabinet because we are  entirely committed  to giving priority to children and young  people's education  you may have heard keir starmer saying  it was his number one priority  in his interview with piers morgan  earlier this week  we've costed these proposals very  carefully if people want to look at our  children's recovery plan they can see  the detail of every element of that plan  and how we've costed it we also know  that the government itself and quite  rightly  has set aside a covered reserve to get  the country back on its feet  after the pandemic what we're not we're  talking about is um  how do we spend money that the  government knows it will have to spend  we're talking about priorities here  so i would say that um it's absolutely  right that we should give  top priority to children and young  people that's our responsibility to them  as adults  but it's also in our interest as a  country because the cost of not spending  14 and a half billion pounds now  in terms of lost earnings lost  productivity in the future will run into  hundreds of billions of pounds and i'm  delighted and very grateful to rachel  and my shadow treasury colleagues  uh for their very very constructive  support and help  just to be a package together clear just  to be absolutely clear this does include  an extended school day um and by the way  are the teaching unions okay with that  more hours  for teachers yes so what we're talking  about is a range of activities at the  end  at the end of the school day some  including sport art drama culture so on  there could be also the opportunity for  some formal teaching and learning  um we're not saying that any teacher  would have to do that and indeed a lot  of the  sports and music and so on might be done  by youth and community groups although  teachers who want to would be able to  and would be paid to do so  uh we have had been in very close  discussions with the the teaching unions  and they  they understand that position we've  agreed that we will  make sure with them that we're tracking  exactly how any extension of school  of activities after the school day  impacts on workloads so that there can  be very quick  um measures taken if it if it creates  unacceptable burdens on teachers  but you know teachers and the teaching  unions are as committed as anyone  to children and young people's futures  more so it's their day job it's what  they care about  and when i hear the government  rubbishing teachers rubbishing the  teaching unions  rubbishing the professionals who've  worked so hard over the last year  to support our children and young people  when i know that we have had the most  constructive and helpful discussions  with them  frankly i think it's disgraceful all  right i think  let's just talk about keep talking about  the money for for the moment  i see this morning that labor is saying  that  the um tax deal uh  navigated by rishi sunak should  provide dividends for health and  education um  now you've already given boris johnson a  pretty big tick on his um  uh jabbing the world proposition are you  going to congratulate rishi sunak  this morning on um his triumph because  usually it's  it's labor who's calling for crackdown  on tech multinationals and  uh transnationals and uh the the richest  in the world  and they've seen he seems to have done  it  labour very much supported joe biden's  call for a global tax floor of 21  so it's it's a first step but i must say  it's disappointing  uh that we have achieved only 15 coming  out of the g7 this weekend and i know  that that is because rishi sunak and the  conservative government in this country  were not enthusiastic were not prepared  to back  joe biden's call for a rate of 21  percent  so i i think it's good i think it's good  that the g7 collectively have agreed  to 15 i think it's a start i think it's  disappointing that we didn't have a more  ambitious  21 percent agreed that many of the g7  countries but sadly not rishi soon act  wanted to  but at least we've made a start and that  will bring hundreds of billions of  pounds into our exchequer

